per-checkpoint refs under refs/entire/checkpoints/<shard>/<id>, the layout the
git-refs checkpoint store uses.

Each checkpoint's current tree is wrapped in a fresh commit and its ref is
pointed at it — existing branch commits are not remapped. The checkpoint's
metadata is normalized for the new layout: the legacy checkpoint_version field
is dropped and session file paths are rewritten relative to the ref. The
command is idempotent: checkpoints already converted are skipped, so it is safe
to re-run after more branch activity.

Each checkpoint is taken from whichever copy of the v1 branch, this clone's or
a remote's, changed it last. When both changed it since they split, neither
copy can become the ref without hiding the other's sessions, so in a terminal
the command asks which copy to keep; without one it writes no ref for that
checkpoint, lists it, and exits 1.

After the switch to the git-refs store the refs are authoritative, so the
command only resolves checkpoints that still have no ref because their copies
differ. Saving a session or attaching one to such a checkpoint fails until it
is resolved. A ref a remote already holds is fetched instead of asking.

New refs are queued for push. Run interactively, it asks whether to push them
now; non-interactively it never pushes — the refs stay queued and flush on the
next push once the git-refs store is the configured primary.

Usage:
  entire doctor migrate-checkpoints [flags]

Flags:
      --dry-run         Report what would be migrated without writing refs
  -h, --help            help for migrate-checkpoints
      --remote string   Remote to push migrated refs to (default: the checkpoint sync remote)

Global Flags:
      --context name   Act as this saved login name for this command only — including the git and agent processes it spawns — instead of the active context (entire auth contexts lists them)
Update non-agent Entire settings in the current repository.

Manages telemetry, git-hook installation mode, strategy options, and summary
provider configuration. Agent installation is handled by 'entire agent'.

Examples:
  entire configure                                # Show this help
  entire configure --telemetry=false              # Opt out of telemetry
  entire configure --absolute-git-hook-path       # Reinstall git hook with absolute path
  entire configure --force                        # Reinstall git hook
  entire configure --checkpoint-remote github:org/checkpoints   # or gitlab:org/checkpoints
  entire configure --checkpoint-backend refs      # Move a legacy repo to per-checkpoint git refs
  entire configure --checkpoint-push-policy manual   # Keep checkpoints local until 'entire checkpoint push'
  entire configure --summarize-provider claude-code
  entire configure --summarize-timeout-seconds 300   # 5m deadline for explain --generate

Usage:
  entire configure [flags]

Flags:
      --absolute-git-hook-path          Embed full binary path in git hooks (for GUI git clients that don't source shell profiles)
      --checkpoint-backend string       Checkpoint storage backend: refs (default; one git ref per checkpoint) or branch (legacy shared entire/checkpoints/v1 branch)
      --checkpoint-push-policy string   When checkpoints upload for this clone: automatic (on git push) or manual (only with 'entire checkpoint push'). Applies to the whole clone; --local and --project don't change where it is saved
